[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) was marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Its reports describe three
problems, and each now has its own ticket. Engineering closed #4471 on 5 October: the parser is
fixed ("fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales") and the booking-v3 form is retired, so its hint
won't be fixed.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is closed, but the one-day moves are not fixed. Neither change touches them; their ticket stays open.
</Callout>

## What #4471 holds

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The cause comes from the tenant (its locale and booking form, `#lib/kb/locales`), not from the
intake tag: intake guesses from the reporter's browser and is wrong in both directions. Counts
follow the desk's view of the export, by tenant. The `shift` row is the reports the desk itself
read as a one-day move (`desk-notes.json`); they stay counted where they're filed.

## The three problems

- **`parser`**: the shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes
  the day first, so any date with a day of 12 or less comes out flipped: 7 April becomes 4 July.
  Fixed on 5 October.
- **`hint`**: the booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at a day-first
  tenant type what the hint says, and the form reads it the tenant's way. The date flips again,
  through a different path. The parser fix does not touch it. Won't fix: the form is retired.
- **`shift`**: a date that moved by one day, not a flipped one. The same meeting shows as Tuesday in
  Lisbon and Monday in Toronto; an invite arrives before its booking. This points at time zones,
  not at day and month order. Still open.

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line alone. "Wrong day on the invite" came in for
all three problems, so all three landed in #4471. The
[triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) already runs the cause check; it was not used for this ticket.

